note that computing seven item fit statistics for each of 14 items yields a total of $7\cdot14=98$ instances where the researcher needs to evaluate whether an observed value constitutes an anomaly (wether guided by a $p$-value or not). Using an implementation in SAS \cite{christensen2006_SAS,Christensen2013}, the simulation study was conducted by simulating 4000 data sets under the Rasch model and, for each of these, performing the following steps:

\begin{itemize}
\item[(0)] Sampling $N$ values from the empirical distribution of person location estimates.
\item[(i)] estimating item parameters using pairwise conditional estimation \cite{Zwinderman_1995,Andrich2003}
\item[(ii)] estimating person parameters using Weighted Maximum Likelihood \cite{warm},
\item[(iii)] computing item fit statistics based on the response residuals [formula (\ref{responseresiduals})],
\end{itemize}

these steps were repeated for the 11 sample sizes $N=35,70,105,140,210,350,700,1050,1400,2100,3500$ and the empirical distribution for each of these $7\cdot14\cdot11=1078$ combinations are shown on
