\section{Example II: Parametric bootstrapping illustration}

An empirical data set is used to illustrate the problem associated with the use of critical values in practice. The empirical data example used in this study is from the Patient Health Questionnaire (PHQ-9), which was validated using the Rasch model in a sample of 1531 (80.4\% men) heart patients in Denmark by Pedersen et al., \cite{pedersen2016}.
The PHQ-9 is a widely used nine item measure of depression based on the diagnostic criteria of the DSMIV \cite{kroenke2003patient}.

\begin{enumerate}
\item Little interest or pleasure in doing things
\item Feeling down, depressed, or hopeless
\item Trouble falling or staying asleep, or sleeping too much
\item Feeling tired or having little energy
\item Poor appetite or overeating
\item Feeling bad about yourself - or that you are a failure or have let yourself or your family down
\item Trouble concentrating on things, such as reading the newspaper or watching television
\item Moving or speaking so slowly that other people could have noticed. Or the opposite -- being fidgety or restless and moving around a lot more than usual
\item Thoughts that you would be better off dead or of hurting yourself in some way
\end{enumerate}

All PHQ-9 items are rated on a four point Likert scale.

The final columns of Table XX show the fit statistics for the full sample with 1531 respondents and indicates that there are four misfitting items based on the fit residual statistic with a critical value of +/- 2.5 and three items for the Chi-square and ANOVA after using a Bonferroni correction. Table XX reports the fit statistics used in this study for different sample sizes that were selected randomly from the Pedersen et al., sample. It is clear from the table that the number of items that do not fit the model increases as the sample size increases.

Insert table with different items misfitting based on different sample sizes here.

A parametric bootstrapping simulation was performed based on the item parameters and the sample distribution in the Pedersen et al., data.


Using an implementation in SAS \cite{christensen2006_SAS,Christensen2013}, the simulation study was conducted by simulating 10,000 data sets under the Rasch model and, for each of these, performing the following steps:


\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] estimating item parameters using pairwise conditional estimation \cite{Zwinderman_1995,Andrich2003}
\item[(ii)] estimating person parameters using Weighted Maximum Likelihood \cite{warm},
\item[(iii)] computing item fit statistics based on the response residuals [formula (\ref{responseresiduals})],
\item[(iv)] computing item fit statistics based on the score residuals [formula (\ref{scoreresiduals})].
\end{itemize}


