\section{Discussion}


In the currently available implementations the item fit statistics are based on residuals computed for each person-item interaction. This has been criticized by Karabatsos \cite{Karabatsos2000a}, who provided a critical analysis of the residual fit statistics of the Rasch model and argued that

\begin{quote}
faulty statistical properties of the residual fit statistics do not allow either a convenient or a straightforward approach to Rasch fit analysis
\end{quote}

However the use of these item fit statistics persists, even though versions of them with known asymptotic properties are easily derived \cite{Christensen-Kreiner2013}.

Smith illustrated a different problem \cite{smith2008}

For Chi-square and ANOVA only large values indicate an anomaly - we do not know what is wrong

Both ends should be considered for infit and outfit - this is something that is often not done - only the large values are considered indicates of misfit (and also FitResid)

statistical properties are known \cite{Christensen-Kreiner2013}. The practicality of this option is limited due to the lack of implementation in the popular Rasch analysis software packages. It is not well-established how this approach work for small sample sizes.


R packages

Specifically, critical upper values for the outfit have been given as $1+2/\sqrt{N}$ and critical lower values for the infit have been given as $1+6/\sqrt{N}$ \cite{Smith-Schumacker-Bush}.

\subsection{Tabulating critical values}

A solution that would likely be preferred by many practitioners would be the publication of critical values for a range of combinations of sample size, number of items, etc.\ this has been provided by our simulation studies and and interactive tabulation is available at


Something about R Smiths sample size adjustment formulas 0.7-1.3 for the INFIT when N=44, and 0.7-1.3 for the OUTFIT when N=400 in relation to our results

\subsection{Bootstrap approaches}


Bootstrap approaches have been discussed earlier for the the infit and outfit mean square error statistics available in winsteps \cite{Su-Sheu-Wang2007} and a bootstrap approach that requires the combined us of SAS and WINSTEPS has been proposed \cite{Wolfe2008}

The results showed that rule-of-thumb critical values for assessing the magnitude of misfit were not applicable because the infit and outfit mean square error statistics showed different magnitudes of variability over testing conditions


\subsection{Implementing statistically sound fit statistics}

Karabatsos stressed the need to implement residual-free Rasch fit statistics. A lot of earlier work in the psychometric literature has done this \cite{van-den-wollenberg,Glas1988,Christensen-Kreiner2013}, but to date these approaches are not implemented in the most widely used software packages used for Rasch analysis. Before these approaches become commonly adopted, we offer a resource to help put observed fit statistics into context in order to determine whether an apparent anomaly is statistically relevant.

(perhaps put something about these anomalies being further assessed contextually and conceptually before deciding whether to reject).



